<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kamakura shogunate</h1><p class="st-meta">1185 CE – 1333 CE · capital Kamakura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Japan's first warrior government, which ruled from Kamakura alongside the imperial court and twice repelled Mongol invasions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> won the right in 1185 to appoint military governors (shugo) and estate stewards (jito) across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, creating a warrior administration based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>. The court named him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1192.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo's</a> death power passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hojo</a> family, who ruled as regents (shikken) for puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>. They defeated the retired emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-toba">Go-Toba</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jokyu War</a> of 1221 and issued the Goseibai shikimoku law code in 1232 for warriors. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-tokimune">Hojo Tokimune</a> the shogunate resisted Mongol invasions in 1274 and 1281. New Buddhist movements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Honen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a> and Zen spread widely.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The cost of the Mongol defence left warriors unrewarded and resentful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hojo</a>, and in 1333 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitta-yoshisada">Nitta Yoshisada</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>, ending the regime and opening the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenmu-restoration">Kenmu Restoration</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kamakura-shogunate.jpg" alt="Kamakura shogunate" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Artanisen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Provinces_of_Japan_Ryoseikoku_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1185 CE – 1333 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kamakura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Minamoto no Yoritomo, Hojo Yasutoki, Hojo Tokimune</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kamakura bakufu, Kamakura Shogunate</dd></dl></aside></div>
